.Dd $Mdocdate: November 9 2026 $
.Dt HWFAN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm hwfan
.Nd Fan control daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Bk -words
.Op Fl c Ar config_file
.Op Fl vn
.Op Fl h

.Ek
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the daemon program that monitors hardware temperatures and sets
fan speeds using Linux hwmon
.Xr sysfs 5
interface.

The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c Ar config_file
Read configuration from this file instead of default
.Pa /etc/hwfan.conf .
.Pp
.It Fl v
Verbose mode. The daemon writes messages to standard error noting
what temperatures it reads and what fan speeds it sets.
.Pp
.It Fl n
Dry-run mode. The daemon will not set the fan speed, only read
temperatures and print what it will do. Enables verbose mode as with
.Fl v .
.It Fl h
Show short help message.

.Ek
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Nm
reads configuration data from
.Pa /etc/hwfan.conf
(or the file specified with
.Fl c
option on the command line).

The file contains keyword-argument sequences, one per line. Unless
noted otherwise, for each keyword, the last obtained value will be
used. Empty lines and all text following ‘#’ are interpreted as
comments. Arguments are separated by whitespace. Quoting is not
supported as names in hwmon sysfs tree should, generally not contain
any whitespace.

Regular expressions, where used are POSIX extended regular
expressions matched using
.Xr regcomp 3
and
.Xr regexec 3
functions. Keep in mind that regex matching is non-greedy, meaning
that regular expression "pwm[0-9]+" will match both "pwm1" and
"pwm1_enable" files. To make regex match entire word surround it in
beginning/ending of line regex match characters, e.g. for example
above writing "^pwm[0-9]+$" will only match "pwm1".

The possible keywords and their meanings are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm name Ar name
Specifies name to match hwmon interface with.
.It Cm sensor_regex Ar regex
Specifies regular expression used to match files from which
temperature will be read. Temperature is expected to be in
millidegrees. When regex matches multiple files all of them will be
read and max value will be used.
.It Cm pwm_regex Cm Ar regex
Specifies regular expression used to match files to which
.Ar speed
value configured using
.Cm state
directive below will be written to set fan speed.
.It Cm state Ar speed t_low t_high
Specifies temperature states;
.Nm
switches to the next state when
.Ar t_high
temperature is reached and goes back to previous state when
temperature goes back to
.Ar t_low .
Switching states is accomplished by writing values specified in
.Ar speed
plus a newline to all files that were matched by
.Cm pwm_regex
above.

At lest 2 states must be configured.
.Ar t_low
and
.Ar t_high
are ignored in the first/last states as there is no state
following them to.

.Ek
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following example configuration sets up manual fan control for
Dell laptop using dell_smm hwmon entry. This is also a default
configuration that should be accessible at
.Pa /etc/hwfan.conf
on most installations.

.Bl -item -compact -offset indent
.It
name dell_smm
.It
sensor_regex ^temp[0-9]_input$
.It
pwm_regex ^pwm[0-9]+$
.It
# The fan switches to medium speed when reaching temperature
.It
# of 65 degrees, then to max speed on 75 degrees. It goes down
.It
# to medium speed when reaching 60 degrees and disables fans
.It
# after reaching 45 degrees.
.It
state 0   -1 65
.It
state 128 45 75
.It
state 255 60 128
.El

.Ek
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/hwfan.conf
Default configuration file location.

.Ek
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sysfs 5
